{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nBugs are exempt from the claim-time plan-review gate (correct: no RCA exists at claim), and since epic 6982/R4 an explicit 'review-plan <bug>' is no longer a hollow exempt-PASS — it runs the LIGHT ADVISORY bug tier: the DET floor + the necessity probe only (workflow_ops.py bug branch; registry.BUG_TIER_CRITERIA restricts the LLM criteria), with every DET finding downgraded to advisory (det_blocking=[]) so a bug always PASSes. That tier is right-sized for small fixes but means a complex fix plan is never substantively reviewed against the full rubric before landing — fixes shipping design changes (backtest: 13/113 bug-fix commits exceeded 150 non-test lines; all 13 adjudicated substantive, two shipped ADRs) flow to main with at most advisory findings.\n\n## Approach\nB1 (extend the bug review tier, not narrow an exemption): in the workflow_ops.py bug branch + orchestrator.route_criteria (~:190) — the SINGLE routing seam that keys the bug tier (registry.BUG_TIER_CRITERIA restriction at ~:221) and is reached by BOTH the assemble step and the batch-runner's project-criteria fan-in, so escalation lands once and covers both callers —, key the tier on fix-plan size, derived DETERMINISTICALLY at review time (no diff exists yet): escalate iff an explicit --size-lines <n> flag supplies n>150, OR (no flag) the ticket's recorded file_impact enumerates any non-test path (tests/**, conftest.py excluded) — file_impact is the plan's own declared blast radius and needs no diff. Neither signal present -> light advisory tier (deterministic default; B2 at Gerrit backstops with the REAL diff count). When escalated, an explicit 'review-plan <bug>' includes the FULL LLM criteria set (not just BUG_TIER_CRITERIA) and stops forcing det_blocking=[] — findings can BLOCK. Small fixes keep the light advisory tier unchanged. The CLI claim-time bug exemption (rebar._commands.gates._PLAN_REVIEW_EXEMPT_TYPES, consumed by plan_review_precheck) is untouched — bugs stay claimable with no attestation.\nB2: DET code-review criterion at Gerrit: commit trailer ticket is type=bug AND non-test diff >150 lines AND plan_review_status verdict is in the FLAG bucket -> BLOCKING finding teaching remediation (write fix plan into description -> review-plan -> re-push). Non-test line classification rule, load-bearing for both the gate and the backtest: a diff line is \"test\" iff its file path matches tests/** or conftest.py; everything else (src, scripts, workflows, docs) counts as non-test. Verdict buckets. Enum source of truth is EVERY verdict plan_review_status (attest_gate.py) can return — it is strictly wider than compute_validity: on an unverified signature claim_gate_check forwards signing.verify_signature's RAW verdict verbatim (attest_gate.py ~:120-125; literal set at types.py:261-271) BEFORE compute_validity runs, and the lifecycle denial path defaults to 'stale' (attest_gate.py ~:143). So: ACCEPTED = {certified, stale-code, stale-head} (code drift from implementing the reviewed plan is expected — prevents the attestation being invalidated by the fix change itself). FLAG = every verdict that is a deterministic property of THE ATTESTATION ITSELF being untrustworthy or stale: compute_validity's {unsigned, stale-material, stale-reopened, wrong-kind, malformed-pin, malformed-phase, incompatible-phase, stale-pin-drift, stale-pin-missing, unverifiable-material, unknown_scheme, stale} PLUS the signature-authenticity layer {mismatch, foreign_key, key_not_valid_at_era, invalid, unknown_kind} — tamper/authenticity signals are gate signal, NOT infra, and must not fail open. INFRA-FAIL-OPEN = verdicts about the VERIFYING HOST rather than the change: {error, unavailable (missing ssh-keygen on the review-bot host)} plus caught read/exception paths plus any verdict string in NO list (future enum growth): emit a non-blocking advisory naming the infra failure or unknown verdict, never BLOCK on infrastructure. A unit test asserts ACCEPTED ∪ FLAG ∪ INFRA-NAMED exactly covers compute_validity's PLAN-REVIEW-REACHABLE literals ∪ the types.py verify_signature Verdict literals ∪ {'stale', 'error'} — explicitly EXCLUDING 'not-closed' (attest.py ~:666), which only the kind='completion-verifier' branch can return and B2's kind='plan-review' read can never see; the exclusion is stated in the test beside the literal list. Enum growth surfaces as a failing test, not silent fail-open. The 150 threshold lives in ONE shared module-level constant in rebar (beside the bug-tier routing it keys, e.g. the registry/criteria module) imported by BOTH consumers — B1's workflow_ops bug branch and B2's review-bot criterion (the bot imports rebar) — with the backtest citing the same constant; not config-plumbed (two consumers, one constant; config only if an operator-variable appears). plan_review_status (attest_gate.py) is read-only/no-LLM, callable from the review-bot checkout. Works for non-skill clients (keys only on ticket type + diff size + attestation state).\n\n## Scope\n- src/rebar/_cli/_llm_commands.py (the --size-lines flag on review-plan) + the llm.review_plan() facade / review-workflow input threading so the size value reaches plan_review_precheck's ctx.inputs (today the facade passes only ref/source/sign/force)\n- src/rebar/llm/plan_review/workflow_ops.py (bug branch: size-keyed tier escalation) + criteria routing for the escalated path\n- review-bot DET criterion (vote pipeline)\n- rebar-debug skill Phase-1-exit hook: fix-plan-to-description + review-plan for non-trivial fixes (coordinate with the debug-skill child 78f1 — writeback anchor points land there; this ticket only adds the review-plan invocation sentence)\n\n## Testing\nB1: review-plan on a bug with --size-lines 160 (or non-test file_impact) runs the full rubric with blocking enabled (not the light tier); no flag + no file_impact -> light tier; test-only file_impact -> light tier; claim path unchanged (bug claims stay ungated). B2 unit tests over the predicate: bug+160 non-test lines+unsigned -> BLOCK; same with certified or stale-code or stale-head -> no finding; error/read-exception -> advisory only (fail-open); unknown verdict string -> advisory only; task ticket -> no finding; bug+40 lines -> no finding; test-vs-non-test classification cases (tests/** excluded, scripts/ counted). B2 integration: one review-bot pipeline-level test exercising the criterion end-to-end against a fixture checkout (attestation present vs absent), not just the predicate function.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Explicit review-plan <bug> escalates to the full blocking rubric when the size trigger is met, keeps the light advisory tier otherwise; claim-time exemption intact.\n- [ ] Gerrit DET criterion blocks oversized bug fixes lacking a current-or-code-drifted attestation, with a teaching message.\n- [ ] Verdict predicate buckets the FULL plan_review_status surface: accepts certified/stale-code/stale-head; FLAGs all other staleness/malformation verdicts AND the signature-authenticity layer (mismatch, foreign_key, key_not_valid_at_era, invalid, unknown_kind); fails OPEN (advisory, never block) only on error/unavailable/read-failure/unknown-future; coverage unit test pins ACCEPTED ∪ FLAG ∪ INFRA-NAMED = compute_validity's plan-review-reachable literals (excluding completion-verifier-only 'not-closed', exclusion stated in-test) ∪ verify_signature Verdict literals ∪ {stale, error}.\n- [ ] Non-test line classification rule stated and tested; threshold is a single named constant.\n- [ ] Backtest property, exact set equality against the hand-adjudicated corpus: scripts/backtest_bugfix_size.py (one-shot, committed with the change) iterates the 113 historical bug-fix commits, applies the non-test classification rule + the shared 150 constant, and writes a JSON artifact (commit, non-test lines, flagged) recorded on this ticket; PASS iff the flagged set == the 13 commits adjudicated substantive in the planning corpus and none of the remaining 100 (which include every mechanical >150 case: rename/move/format-only/generated churn) is flagged. 'Mechanical FP' means exactly: a flagged commit outside the adjudicated-substantive 13.\n- [ ] Debug-skill Phase-1-exit hook sentence landed (review-plan invocation for non-trivial fixes), coordinated with 78f1."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785774627829210001,"uuid":"9e69a2fb-f4d3-418a-84ed-f491280ede64"}